# Batch 12 (2026-10-24): Celtic Ireland/Britain/Brittany, the Baltic and Finnic world, Armenia and Georgia,
# Siberia/Mongolia/Tibet, West and Central Africa, the Andes beyond the Inca, Byzantium, the Renaissance,
# early-modern Asia, Polynesian 'mystery islands' and deep-time science surprises
H,M='history','myth'
def X(id,title,lane,status,pr,start,ev,main,alts,src,img=None,end=None,changed=None,dateNote=None):
  d=dict(id=id,title=title,lane=lane,status=status,priority=pr,start=start,end=end,ev=ev,main=main,alts=alts,src=src,img=img)
  if changed:d['changed']=changed
  if dateNote:d['dateNote']=dateNote
  return d
E=[
# --- part 1: Celtic Ireland, Britain and Brittany
X('tuatha-de-danann','The Tuatha Dé Danann: Ireland\'s gods who arrived in dark clouds and went into the hills',M,'myth-legend',2,1050,['textual (medieval Irish)','mythological'],
 "The Lebor Gabála Érenn (\"Book of the Taking of Ireland\"), compiled by Christian monks in the 11th century from older material, tells Ireland's history as a series of invasions: Cessair before the Flood, then Partholón, Nemed, the Fir Bolg, the Tuatha Dé Danann (\"peoples of the goddess Danu\") and finally the Milesians, ancestors of the Gaels. The Tuatha Dé arrive in dark clouds, bringing four treasures (the Stone of Fál, the spear of Lugh, the sword of Nuada and the Dagda's cauldron). Defeated by the Milesians, they withdraw into the síde, the ancient mounds such as Newgrange, and become the aos sí, the fairy folk of later Irish belief. Most scholars see them as the pre-Christian gods of Ireland, recast by monastic writers as a human or semi-human people so they could fit into a biblical history of the world.",
 [('Gods made human','The mainstream reading is euhemerism: the monks turned the old gods (Lugh, the Dagda, the Morrígan, Brigid) into early kings and magicians and dated them alongside Noah and Moses.'),
  ('Memory of real invasions','19th-century antiquarians tried to match the invasions to real peoples (for example the Fir Bolg to the Belgae); modern archaeology and genetics show real population changes in Ireland, but they do not map onto the medieval scheme, so this remains speculative.'),
  ('Sky arrival','Some ancient-astronaut writers read the arrival \"in dark clouds\" or in ships through the air as a description of aircraft; this is speculative, and other versions simply say they burned their ships on landing so the smoke hid the sun.')],
 ['wiki:Tuatha_Dé_Danann','wiki:Lebor_Gabála_Érenn'],'Tuatha_Dé_Danann',dateNote='Text compiled 11th century CE; legendary setting before c. 1000 BCE'),
X('brendan-voyage','The voyage of Saint Brendan: Irish monks across the Atlantic in a leather boat?',M,'disputed',2,900,['textual (Latin, medieval)','experimental voyage'],
 "Brendan of Clonfert (c. 484 – c. 577) was a real Irish abbot. The Navigatio Sancti Brendani Abbatis (\"Voyage of Saint Brendan the Abbot\"), a Latin text probably written in the 9th or 10th century, tells how he and a group of monks sailed a hide-covered boat (a currach) for seven years in search of the Promised Land of the Saints. They celebrate Easter on the back of a huge fish, Jasconius, which they first take for an island, pass a floating crystal column, and are pelted with burning rocks by smiths on a dark island. It was hugely popular in medieval Europe, and 'Saint Brendan's Island' appeared on Atlantic charts for centuries. Irish hermits (papar) really did reach the Faroes and Iceland before the Norse, according to the Irish scholar Dicuil (825) and the Icelandic Íslendingabók.",
 [('A real crossing','In 1976–77 the explorer Tim Severin built a leather currach and sailed from Ireland to Newfoundland via the Hebrides, Faroes and Iceland, showing that such a voyage was technically possible; some readers see the crystal column as an iceberg and the smiths\' island as an Icelandic eruption.'),
  ('Religious allegory','Most medievalists read the Navigatio as a spiritual allegory built around the monastic calendar, drawing on the older Irish immram (voyage) tales; possibility is not evidence that Brendan himself reached America.'),
  ('Pre-Columbian claims','The story is often cited alongside Vinland in debates about pre-Columbian Atlantic crossings; there is no archaeological trace of Irish monks in North America.')],
 ['wiki:Brendan_the_Navigator','wiki:Immram'],'Brendan_the_Navigator',dateNote='Brendan lived c. 484–577; the Navigatio was written c. 800–1000 CE'),
X('hy-brasil','Hy-Brasil: the phantom island west of Ireland that stayed on maps for 500 years',M,'myth-legend',2,1325,['cartographic','folklore','textual'],
 "Hy-Brasil is a phantom island said to lie in the Atlantic west of Ireland. Irish tradition described it as hidden in mist except for one day every seven years, when it can be seen but not reached. An island called \"Bracile\" appears west of Ireland on Angelino Dulcert's portolan chart of about 1325 and on the Catalan Atlas (c. 1375); later charts usually drew it as a circle split by a channel. Expeditions sailed from Bristol to look for it in 1480 and 1481. In 1674 a Captain John Nisbet supposedly landed there and found giant black rabbits and a magician in a stone castle, but the account was a literary invention by the Irish writer Richard Head. It kept appearing on charts south-west of Galway Bay until 1865, by which time it had shrunk to \"Brasil Rock\". The name may come from the Irish Uí Breasail (a clan name) or be linked to the medieval red dye brasile; the country Brazil was later named after brazilwood.",
 [('Misread shoals or mirages','Suggested real origins include the Porcupine Bank, a shallow area west of Ireland, or optical effects such as fata morgana that can make distant land appear over the horizon.'),
  ('Otherworld island','Folklorists link it to the Irish otherworld islands such as Tír na nÓg, where time runs differently, and to the immram voyage tales.'),
  ('Found America first?','A letter written shortly after John Cabot\'s 1497 voyage reports that the land Cabot found had been \"discovered in the past by the men from Bristol who found Brasil\"; some historians suggest Bristol sailors searching for the island reached Newfoundland before John Cabot\'s 1497 voyage, while others think the letter refers only to the search. The question is unresolved.')],
 ['wiki:Hy-Brasil','wiki:Phantom_island'],'Hy-Brasil',end=1865,dateNote='On sea charts c. 1325 – 1865'),
X('gundestrup-cauldron','The Gundestrup cauldron: a horned god, a giant dipping men into a cauldron, and elephants in a Danish bog',H,'confirmed',2,-100,['archaeological','iconographic'],
 "The Gundestrup cauldron is a richly decorated silver vessel found in 1891 by peat cutters in a bog near Gundestrup in Himmerland, Denmark, where it had been taken apart and deposited, probably as an offering. It is the largest known piece of European Iron Age silverwork (about 69 cm across) and is usually dated between about 150 BCE and 1 BCE. Its panels show a seated antlered figure holding a torc and a ram-horned serpent, often identified with the Gaulish god Cernunnos; a giant figure plunging a man head-first into a vat while warriors march past; wheel-holding deities; and exotic animals including elephants and lion-like beasts. The workmanship resembles Thracian silverwork from south-east Europe, while much of the imagery looks Celtic, so it is a meeting point of several cultures. It is in the National Museum of Denmark.",
 [('Sacrifice or rebirth','The vat scene has been read as a human sacrifice by drowning (a later commentary on the Roman poet Lucan says victims were drowned for the god Teutates) or as soldiers being brought back to life in a cauldron of rebirth, as in the Welsh Mabinogion.'),
  ('Who made it','Scholars debate whether Thracian silversmiths made it for Celtic patrons, whether it was booty, or whether it was made in Gaul; elephants suggest knowledge of the Mediterranean or the East.'),
  ('Shamanic imagery','Some see the antlered figure as a shaman or a lord of animals comparable to images from India (the so-called Pashupati seal of the Indus civilisation); such links are speculative.')],
 ['wiki:Gundestrup_cauldron'],'Gundestrup_cauldron',dateNote='Made c. 150 – 1 BCE; found 1891'),
X('ogham','Ogham: Ireland\'s notched alphabet, invented by a god, and the ogham claims in America',H,'confirmed',3,350,['inscriptions','textual'],
 "Ogham is an early medieval alphabet made of groups of strokes and notches cut along a central line, usually the edge of a standing stone. About 400 inscriptions survive, mostly in Ireland and also in Wales, Scotland, the Isle of Man and Cornwall; the oldest are usually dated from about the 4th to the 6th centuries CE. Most record a personal name in the form \"X son of Y\", probably marking land boundaries or graves. Medieval Irish texts such as the Auraicept na nÉces say ogham was invented by Ogma, one of the Tuatha Dé Danann, and name its letters after trees. Scholars think it was designed by people who knew the Latin alphabet and Latin grammar, and adapted to the sounds of Primitive Irish.",
 [('Divine invention','In Irish tradition Ogma created ogham to give learned men a secret script; the stories also describe it being used for magical messages carved on wood.'),
  ('Ogham in America','In America B.C. (1976) the marine biologist Barry Fell claimed that markings on rocks in West Virginia and elsewhere were ogham left by ancient Irish or Celtic visitors; archaeologists and Celtic scholars reject these readings as natural or later scratches interpreted through wishful thinking.'),
  ('Older origins?','A few writers suggest ogham grew out of an older tally or sign system; there is no surviving evidence of ogham before the Roman-influenced period.')],
 ['wiki:Ogham','wiki:Barry_Fell'],'Ogham',dateNote='Oldest inscriptions c. 4th–6th century CE'),
X('giants-causeway','The Giant\'s Causeway: a road built by Fionn mac Cumhaill, or cooling lava?',H,'confirmed',2,-55000000,['geological','folklore'],
 "The Giant's Causeway on the north coast of County Antrim, Northern Ireland, is made of about 40,000 interlocking basalt columns, most of them hexagonal. They formed about 50–60 million years ago, when thick lava flows cooled slowly and contracted, cracking into columns, during the volcanic activity that accompanied the opening of the North Atlantic. Similar columns appear across the sea at Fingal's Cave on Staffa in Scotland. In legend, the giant Fionn mac Cumhaill (Finn McCool) built the causeway to reach his Scottish rival Benandonner; when Benandonner came across, Fionn's wife Oonagh disguised Fionn as a baby, and the Scot fled, tearing up the causeway behind him. It was announced to the Royal Society in 1693, and Susanna Drury's paintings (1739) made it famous; in 1768 the French geologist Nicolas Desmarest, captioning engravings of it, suggested in print for the first time that such columns were volcanic, part of a long argument over whether basalt formed from lava or precipitated from water.",
 [('Too regular to be natural?','Its geometric regularity long fed the feeling that such columns must be built; the same intuition still drives claims about other columnar basalt sites, such as Nan Madol or Gunung Padang.'),
  ('Physics of cracking','Laboratory work and modelling show that cooling lava naturally cracks into polygons, with hexagons favoured because they release stress efficiently; column size depends on the cooling rate.'),
  ('Giant\'s road','The Fionn story is a comic folktale rather than a creation myth; it links two landscapes across the sea that really do share the same geology.')],
 ['wiki:Giant\'s_Causeway','wiki:Fingal\'s_Cave','url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/369/|UNESCO World Heritage: Giant\'s Causeway and Causeway Coast|heritage'],'Giant\'s_Causeway',dateNote='Basalt formed c. 50–60 million years ago; legend recorded in modern folklore'),
X('druids','The druids: what we really know, and the forgers who reinvented them',H,'disputed',2,-58,['textual (Greek and Roman)','archaeological','later forgeries'],
 "The druids were the learned priestly class of the Iron Age Celtic-speaking peoples of Gaul and Britain. Almost everything known about them comes from outsiders: Julius Caesar (Gallic War, book 6, 50s BCE) says they judged disputes, taught young men orally for up to twenty years, believed souls passed into new bodies after death and met yearly in the land of the Carnutes; he and other writers claim they presided over human sacrifices, including burning victims in huge wicker figures. Pliny the Elder describes them cutting mistletoe from an oak with a golden sickle, and Tacitus describes the Roman attack on their stronghold on Anglesey (Mona) in 60 or 61 CE. The druids left no writing of their own. From the 17th century antiquarians such as John Aubrey and William Stukeley credited them with Stonehenge and Avebury, which are in fact thousands of years older, and in 1792 the Welsh poet Iolo Morganwg founded the Gorsedd, supported partly by manuscripts he had forged.",
 [('Roman propaganda?','Many historians caution that accounts of druidic human sacrifice came from enemies conquering their lands and may be exaggerated; others point to archaeological evidence of ritual killing, such as Lindow Man (found 1984), though linking such bodies to druids is debated.'),
  ('Keepers of lost wisdom','Romantic and modern pagan traditions see druids as guardians of ancient astronomical and spiritual knowledge; much of this tradition was created in the 18th and 19th centuries.'),
  ('Revived druidry','Modern druid orders openly describe their practice as a revival inspired by fragments of history and folklore rather than an unbroken tradition.')],
 ['wiki:Druid','wiki:Iolo_Morganwg','wiki:Lindow_Man'],'Druid',dateNote='Classical accounts c. 58 BCE – 1st century CE; antiquarian revival 17th–19th centuries'),
X('selkies','Selkies: seal-people of the northern isles, and families who claimed seal ancestry',M,'myth-legend',3,1700,['folklore','oral tradition'],
 "Selkies are shapeshifters of the folklore of Orkney, Shetland, the Hebrides, Ireland, Iceland and the Faroe Islands: seals that can shed their skins and walk ashore as humans. In the commonest story, a man steals and hides a seal-woman's skin so she must stay and marry him; years later she or one of her children finds the skin, and she returns to the sea, sometimes still watching over her family. Some families were said to descend from selkies, such as the MacCodrums of North Uist, the 'MacCodrums of the seals'. The Faroese tale of Kópakonan (the seal woman) of Mikladalur is commemorated by a bronze statue in the village. The word comes from Scots selch, a grey seal. Collected mainly in the 18th and 19th centuries, the stories are much older.",
 [('Explaining difference','Folklorists suggest such stories explained unusual traits: the MacCodrum descent story has been linked to webbed fingers (syndactyly) in the family.'),
  ('Kayaking strangers','The antiquarian David MacRitchie (1890s) argued that the stories remembered real people in skin boats, perhaps Sámi or Inuit; there are 17th–18th century reports of lone \'Finnmen\' in kayaks off Orkney and a kayak kept in Aberdeen. Most historians think any such visitors were Inuit taken to Europe by whalers rather than an ancient seafaring people, and the idea remains debated.'),
  ('Human-animal marriage worldwide','The seal-wife tale belongs to the international \'swan maiden\' type, in which a hidden animal skin or feather cloak binds a supernatural wife; similar tales exist in Japan, Siberia and North America.')],
 ['wiki:Selkie','wiki:Swan_maiden'],'Selkie',dateNote='Collected mainly 18th–19th centuries; older oral tradition'),
X('cantre-gwaelod','Cantre\'r Gwaelod: the drowned kingdom of Cardigan Bay and its forest of stumps',M,'myth-legend',2,500,['textual (medieval Welsh)','geological','folklore'],
 "Cantre'r Gwaelod (\"the lowland hundred\") is a legendary kingdom said to lie under Cardigan Bay in west Wales. In the oldest version, a poem in the 13th-century Black Book of Carmarthen, a maiden called Mererid lets a well overflow and the land is drowned. In the better-known later version, the drunken Seithennin neglects the sluice gates of the sea wall during a storm and sixteen towns are lost; their bells are said to ring under the water. At Borth and Ynyslas, storms periodically uncover the stumps of a submerged prehistoric forest of oak, pine and birch, thought to have been submerged about 4,000–5,000 years ago as sea levels rose after the Ice Age. Long stony ridges called sarnau, such as Sarn Badrig, run out into the bay; they are glacial deposits left by retreating ice sheets, though legend called them the kingdom's roads.",
 [('Memory of rising seas','Some researchers suggest the legend preserves a very long memory of land lost to rising sea levels after the last Ice Age, comparable to Aboriginal Australian stories; others think the stories were inspired by the visible stumps and ridges rather than remembered from prehistory.'),
  ('Moral tale','The legend blames a human failing (neglect or drunkenness) for the flood, a theme shared with Ys in Brittany and the biblical flood.'),
  ('Medieval storms','Another view links the legend to real medieval coastal flooding in the 13th–14th centuries, when the story first appears in writing.')],
 ['wiki:Cantre\'r_Gwaelod','wiki:Sarn_Badrig'],'Cantre\'r_Gwaelod',dateNote='Legendary setting c. 5th–6th century CE; forest drowned c. 4,000–5,000 years ago'),
X('ys','Ys: the Breton city drowned when a princess stole the key to the sea gates',M,'myth-legend',2,500,['folklore','textual (early modern)'],
 "Ys (Breton Kêr-Is) is a legendary city on the coast of Brittany, in or near the Bay of Douarnenez, built by King Gradlon below sea level and protected by a dyke whose gates only the king could open. In the best-known version, his daughter Dahut, who lived a life of pleasure, stole the key from her sleeping father for a lover (sometimes the Devil in disguise) and opened the gates; the sea poured in. Gradlon escaped on horseback, but Saint Gwénnolé told him to cast off Dahut, who fell into the waves and became a mermaid (a morgen). The story is attested from the 15th–16th centuries, and Albert Le Grand's lives of the Breton saints (17th century) contain the first known mention of Dahut. It became famous through the Breton song collection Barzaz Breiz (1839). A popular folk etymology said Paris was named Par-Is, 'equal to Ys'.",
 [('Sunken Roman town?','Some local historians have searched for real remains under the bay, citing Roman roads that run into the sea near Douarnenez; no submerged city has been found.'),
  ('Rising seas','As with Cantre\'r Gwaelod in Wales and Lyonesse in Cornwall, some suggest a folk memory of coastal land lost to the sea; the Breton coast has submerged prehistoric sites, but none linked to a city.'),
  ('Christian vs pagan','Scholars read the tale as a story of the Christian saint triumphing over a pagan, sensual princess, a reworking of older water-fairy beliefs.')],
 ['wiki:Ys','wiki:Lyonesse'],'Ys',dateNote='Legendary setting c. 5th–6th century CE; first written versions 15th–17th centuries'),
# --- part 2: the Baltic and Finnic world, Armenia and Georgia
X('kaali-crater','Kaali: a Bronze Age meteorite strike on an Estonian island, and a sacred lake',H,'confirmed',2,-1500,['geological','archaeological','folklore'],
 "Kaali, on the Estonian island of Saaremaa, is a group of nine meteorite craters. The largest is about 110 m across and 22 m deep and holds a small round lake; the other eight are 12–40 m wide and all lie within a kilometre. The latest estimates date the impact to shortly after 1530–1450 BCE, when the island was already inhabited. The impact released energy roughly comparable to the Hiroshima bomb (about 20 kilotons of TNT) and burned forest for several kilometres around. Until the 1930s some thought the crater was volcanic or a sinkhole; Ivan Reinvald showed in 1928–1937 that it was made by a meteorite. Excavations in 1976–79 found a fortified settlement and a stone wall around the lake, with animal bones (horses, dogs, sheep, pigs, oxen) that may be sacrifices, mostly from the pre-Roman Iron Age, so the lake seems to have been a cult site for centuries.",
 [("Remembered in myth","Scholars such as Karl Kello and the writer Lennart Meri suggested that Estonian and Finnish poems about a sun or fire falling from the sky, including the Kalevala episode (runes 47–49) in which a spark of the new Sun falls from the sky, preserve a memory of the impact; this is plausible but cannot be proven."),
  ("Ultima Thule","In his book Hõbevalge (Silverwhite, 1976), Meri, later Estonia's president, speculated that the classical name Thule, the land 'where the sun goes to sleep', came from the Kaali story; most historians place Thule elsewhere (Shetland, Iceland or Norway)."),
  ("Dating debate","Published estimates of the impact date have varied by about 6,000 years, from roughly 6400 BCE to 400 BCE; the Bronze Age date is now most widely accepted.")],
 ['wiki:Kaali_crater','wiki:Lennart_Meri'],'Kaali_crater',dateNote='Impact shortly after c. 1530–1450 BCE (other estimates differ)'),
X('sampo','The Sampo: the Finnish magic mill that made grain, salt and gold, and the theft that broke it',M,'myth-legend',3,1835,['oral poetry (runic songs)','textual'],
 "In Finnish and Karelian runic songs the Sampo is a mysterious object of plenty forged by the smith Ilmarinen, originally a sky god who had also forged the sky. In Elias Lönnrot's Kalevala (1835; expanded 1849), compiled from songs he and others collected, Ilmarinen makes it for Louhi, the mistress of the dark northern land Pohjola, as a bride-price; it grinds out flour, salt and gold. The heroes Väinämöinen, Ilmarinen and Lemminkäinen steal it back, Louhi pursues them, and in the battle at sea the Sampo is smashed; its fragments wash ashore and bring prosperity to Finland. The songs never describe clearly what it looks like, and it has 'a lid of many colours' (kirjokansi).",
 [("World pillar","Uno Harva and others argued that the Sampo was originally a world pillar or world tree holding up the sky, like the Estonian Sammas or the Norse Yggdrasil."),
  ("Many interpretations","It has also been read as the Sun, a compass, a treasure chest, a Byzantine coin die, a decorated shield or a Christian relic; Lönnrot himself made it a mill, perhaps influenced by the Norse magic mill Grótti."),
  ("Cosmic machine","In Hamlet's Mill (1969), Giorgio de Santillana and Hertha von Dechend argued that the Sampo and similar mills encode ancient knowledge of the precession of the equinoxes; most scholars see this as speculative.")],
 ['wiki:Sampo','wiki:Kalevala','wiki:Elias_Lönnrot'],'Sampo',dateNote='Runic songs are older; collected from 1817; Kalevala published 1835 and 1849'),
X('lithuania-last-pagans','Lithuania: the last pagan state in Europe',H,'confirmed',2,1387,['textual (chronicles)','archaeological'],
 "While the rest of Europe had become Christian, the Grand Duchy of Lithuania kept its old religion, with gods such as the thunder god Perkūnas, into the late 14th century, despite crusades by the Teutonic Knights. King Mindaugas was baptised in the 1250s, but the country went back to the old ways after his death. Lithuania officially converted in 1387, after Grand Duke Jogaila married the Polish queen Jadwiga and became king of Poland; nobles and peasants were baptised in and around Vilnius. Samogitia in the west, long held by the Teutonic Order, was the last region to convert: Grand Duke Vytautas baptised the first Samogitians in 1413, after the Battle of Grunwald, and the diocese of Samogitia was founded in 1417. Pagan customs survived among country people for much longer.",
 [("Holy groves and fires","Chronicles describe sacred groves, holy snakes and eternal fires; how organised the pagan religion was (for example whether there was a high priest, the 'Krivis') is debated because much comes from hostile Christian writers."),
  ("Temple under the cathedral","A tradition going back to the 15th-century historian Jan Długosz linked Vilnius Cathedral with an earlier building later used for pagan worship; modern archaeologists and historians question this interpretation."),
  ("Revival","Romuva, a modern revival of Baltic pagan religion, began in the 20th century and draws on folklore and these medieval accounts.")],
 ['wiki:Christianization_of_Lithuania','wiki:Perkūnas','wiki:Vilnius_Cathedral'],'Christianization_of_Lithuania',end=1417,dateNote='Official conversion 1387; Samogitia 1413–1417'),
X('sami-drums','Sámi drums: the shaman drums burned by missionaries, and the one that came home after 300 years',H,'confirmed',2,1693,['material culture','court records','textual'],
 "Sámi noaidi (shamans) used drums painted with figures of gods, animals, people and the landscape to enter trance, contact the spirit world and foretell the future. During the 17th and 18th centuries the Swedish and Danish-Norwegian authorities and church tried to wipe out the old Sámi religion: drums were confiscated and burned, and owners were tried. In Arjeplog, Sweden, Lars Nilsson was tried in 1691 for keeping his drum and his old faith and was burned at the stake in 1693. About 70 drums survive, mostly in museums across Europe. One of the best documented, Freavnantjahke gievrie (the Folldal drum), was taken by the missionary Thomas von Westen in 1723, passed through Copenhagen and ended up in Meiningen, Germany; the Meiningen Museum decided to return it in June 2023 and handed it over to the South Sámi museum Saemien Sijte in October 2023.",
 [("What the symbols mean","Only a few drums were explained by their owners; later scholars such as Ernst Manker interpreted the figures as gods and spirits, while some contemporary explanations relate them to weather, animals and daily life, so readings remain uncertain."),
  ("Witchcraft rumours","During the Thirty Years' War rumours spread that Swedish armies won with the help of Sámi sorcery, part of the background to the state's interest in Sámi religion."),
  ("Living tradition","Many Sámi today see the drums as living cultural heritage rather than museum objects, which is why their return matters.")],
 ['wiki:Sámi_drum','wiki:Lars_Nilsson_(shaman)','wiki:Thomas_von_Westen','url:https://sametinget.no/politikk/taler-og-innlegg/tale-i-overgivelsesseremoni-for-freavnantjahke-gievrie.28092.aspx|Sámi Parliament of Norway: speech at the handover ceremony for Freavnantjahke gievrie (20 Oct 2023)|official'],'Sámi_drum',dateNote='Drum persecution 17th–18th centuries; Lars Nilsson executed 1693; drum returned 2023'),
X('amber-road','The Amber Road: Baltic "tears of the sun" in Mycenae and Tutankhamun\'s tomb',H,'confirmed',3,-1600,['archaeological','chemical analysis','textual'],
 "Amber, fossilised tree resin washed up on the shores of the Baltic and North Seas, was traded south for thousands of years along routes now called the Amber Road. Heinrich Schliemann found amber beads at Mycenae that spectroscopic analysis showed to be Baltic, and a breast ornament of Tutankhamun (c. 1333–1324 BCE) contains large Baltic amber beads. Amber was also sent as an offering to the temple of Apollo at Delphi. In the Roman period a route ran from the Baltic coast to Aquileia on the Adriatic. Greek myth explained amber as the hardened tears of the Heliades, the sisters of Phaethon, who wept after he crashed the sun's chariot. A Lithuanian legend first written down in 1842 tells of the sea goddess Jūratė, whose amber palace under the Baltic was shattered by the thunder god Perkūnas because she loved a mortal fisherman, Kastytis; the pieces of amber on the shore are its ruins.",
 [("Old myth or modern tale","Because Jūratė does not appear in older Lithuanian mythology, scholars think the legend is largely a 19th-century literary creation, although it uses older gods such as Perkūnas."),
  ("Magic and medicine","Amber was widely believed to have protective and healing powers, which helps explain why it travelled so far; amber and other exotic materials in elite graves show long-distance networks long before written records."),
  ("Wider contacts","Some writers use amber finds to argue for direct Bronze Age voyages between the Baltic and the Mediterranean; most archaeologists think the amber moved through many hands in stages.")],
 ['wiki:Amber_Road','wiki:Jūratė_and_Kastytis'],'Amber_Road',dateNote='Baltic amber in the Mediterranean from the 2nd millennium BCE'),
X('areni-1','Areni-1 cave: the world\'s oldest leather shoe and oldest winery, next to the dead',H,'confirmed',2,-4100,['archaeological','chemical analysis','radiocarbon'],
 "Areni-1 is a three-chambered cave by the Arpa River in Vayots Dzor, southern Armenia. Excavations by an Armenian, Irish and American team (Boris Gasparyan, Ron Pinhasi, Gregory Areshian) from 2007 to 2010 found remarkably preserved remains from the Late Chalcolithic, protected by the cave's dry, cool air and layers of sheep dung. In 2008 Diana Zardaryan found a complete one-piece leather shoe, about 5,500 years old (c. 3600–3500 BCE), stuffed with grass and laced, the oldest leather shoe known. The cave also held the oldest known winery, from about 4100–4000 BCE: a clay basin used as a press, a 60 cm fermentation vat, storage jars, grape seeds and skins, with traces of the red-wine pigment malvidin. The winery sat among burials, and a human brain preserved in a skull was reported in 2009.",
 [("Wine for the dead","Because the press and vat were found among graves, the excavators suggested that wine was made for funeral rituals, linking the beginnings of wine with religion rather than everyday drinking."),
  ("Cradle of wine","The South Caucasus (Armenia and Georgia) and eastern Anatolia have produced the earliest evidence of grape wine; Georgian sites have traces from around 6000 BCE, earlier than Areni's winery but without a press installation."),
  ("Same shoe for thousands of years","The shoe resembles one-piece leather shoes known from Europe and the traditional Irish pampootie, suggesting a very long-lived design.")],
 ['wiki:Areni-1_cave','wiki:Areni-1_shoe','wiki:Areni-1_winery','doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0010984','doi:10.1016/j.jas.2010.11.012'],'Areni-1_shoe',dateNote='Winery c. 4100–4000 BCE; shoe c. 3600–3500 BCE'),
X('zorats-karer','Zorats Karer (Carahunge): Armenia\'s "Stonehenge" of holed stones, observatory or necropolis?',H,'disputed',2,-2000,['archaeological','archaeoastronomy'],
 "Zorats Karer, also called Carahunge or Karahunj, is a site on a plateau at about 1,770 m near Sisian in Syunik, southern Armenia. It contains stone settings, burial cists and 223 numbered standing stones of basalt, up to 3 m tall and up to 10 tons. About 80 stones have a circular hole bored through them, and the wind can make them whistle, which fits the popular explanation of the name Carahunge as 'speaking stones'. In 1984 the archaeologist Onik Khnkikyan drew attention to possible astronomical meaning, and from 1985 the astrophysicist Elma Parsamyan and later the radiophysicist Paris Herouni proposed that it was an ancient observatory, with some stones aligned on solstice sunrises and lunar extremes. In 2004 the Armenian government officially named the site the Karahunj Observatory.",
 [("Necropolis and later wall","A German team from LMU Munich led by Stephan Kroll identified the site as a necropolis mainly from the Middle Bronze Age to the Iron Age, with large tombs, and suggested that the lines of stones were remains of a later, possibly Hellenistic, wall."),
  ("Very ancient observatory","Herouni argued that the alignments dated the site to about 5500 BCE, making it older than Stonehenge; archaeoastronomers such as Clive Ruggles have called such claims speculative and poorly supported."),
  ("Holes not prehistoric?","The insides of the holes are much less weathered than the stone surfaces, so some of them may be much later than the stones; conclusions about their use remain provisional.")],
 ['wiki:Carahunge'],'Carahunge',dateNote='Tombs mainly Middle Bronze to Iron Age (c. 2000–800 BCE); observatory dating claims up to c. 5500 BCE'),
X('amirani','Amirani: the Georgian Prometheus chained in the Caucasus',M,'myth-legend',3,-1000,['oral epic','folklore'],
 "Amirani is the hero of a Georgian epic known in many oral versions. Son of Dali, a Caucasian goddess of the hunt, he is a giant-slaying hero who defies God (in some versions by bringing the use of metal to humanity) and is punished by being chained to a stake or beneath a mountain rock in the Caucasus with his winged dog Q'ursha. In some versions an eagle eats his liver, which heals each night. The dog licks the chains until they are about to break, but then blacksmiths strike their anvils and the chains regain their strength; once a year the mountain opens and Amirani may see the sky for a single night.",
 [("Source of Prometheus?","The Greek Prometheus, who brings fire and is chained in the Caucasus with an eagle eating his liver, is strikingly similar; many scholars think the Greeks took the setting, and perhaps elements of the story, from Caucasian peoples."),
  ("Very old roots","The core of the legend has been traced to the 3rd–2nd millennia BCE and the beginning of metalworking; this is hard to prove because the epic was written down only in modern times."),
  ("The hero who will return","Like King Artavazd in the Armenian tradition recorded by Movses Khorenatsi, chained in a cave of Mount Masis (Ararat) whose chains smiths strengthen by striking their anvils, Amirani is a bound hero whose escape is feared or awaited.")],
 ['wiki:Amirani','wiki:Prometheus'],'Amirani',dateNote='Oral epic; core possibly 1st–2nd millennium BCE; recorded from the 19th century'),
X('golden-fleece','The Golden Fleece: Colchis, Jason and the sheepskins used to catch gold',M,'myth-legend',2,-1300,['textual (Greek)','archaeological','ethnographic'],
 "In Greek myth Jason and the Argonauts sailed to Colchis, at the eastern end of the Black Sea in what is now western Georgia, to take the Golden Fleece of a flying ram from King Aeëtes, son of the Sun; the king's daughter, the sorceress Medea, helped him. The story is told most fully by Apollonius of Rhodes in the 3rd century BCE, though it is much older. Colchis really was rich in gold: the Greek geographer Strabo (1st century CE) wrote that in the country of the Soani (Svans) gold was carried down by mountain torrents and caught with perforated troughs and fleecy skins, and suggested this was the origin of the myth. Archaeologists have found rich gold jewellery in Colchian graves such as those at Vani.",
 [("Gold-washing fleece","The mainstream rational explanation follows Strabo: sheepskins laid in streams trapped gold dust, a method reportedly used in Svaneti into modern times."),
  ("Real voyages","Some historians see the myth as a memory of early Greek trading or exploring voyages into the Black Sea; in 1984 Tim Severin rowed a replica Bronze Age galley from Greece to Georgia to show it was possible."),
  ("Other readings","The fleece has also been read as a symbol of kingship, a sun symbol, a record of writing on parchment, or alchemical gold; these are interpretations rather than evidence.")],
 ['wiki:Golden_Fleece','wiki:Colchis'],'Golden_Fleece',dateNote='Legendary voyage set before the Trojan War (traditional c. 13th century BCE); fullest text 3rd century BCE'),
X('vishap-stones','Vishap stones: Armenia\'s fish- and bull-shaped dragon stelae high in the mountains',H,'confirmed',2,-4100,['archaeological','folklore'],
 "Vishapakar ('dragon stones') are tall stone stelae found in the Armenian Highlands, mostly near springs, streams, ancient irrigation channels and mountain lakes, including on the slopes of Mount Aragats and in the Gegham mountains. About 150 are known, 90 of them in Armenia, and the largest is just over 5 m tall. Most are carved as a fish, often like a catfish, with eyes, gills and tail; others show the stretched hide of a bull or ram, sometimes with water flowing from its mouth, and long-legged birds. Armenian folklore calls them vishaps, water dragons. Their age is hard to establish because they stand far from settlements; one was reused for an inscription of the Urartian king Argishti I (8th century BCE), and later ones were carved with Christian crosses, so they are older than both.",
 [("Water cult","In the 1920s–30s Ashkharbek Kalantar linked them to irrigation and a cult of water; a 2025 statistical study of 115 stones by Vahe Gurzadyan and Arsen Bobokhyan found larger stones at high altitudes where little time was available for work, which they took as support for a strong cult motive."),
  ("6,000 years old","Excavations since 2012 at Tirinkatar on Mount Aragats, at about 2,700–3,100 m, radiocarbon-dated the erection of two vishaps to about 4200–4000 BCE; the excavators say this cannot automatically be extended to all vishaps, which were also reused in later periods."),
  ("Dragons and water","The idea of a water-holding dragon or serpent appears in many cultures, from the Vedic Vritra to Chinese rain dragons, which some see as a shared motif and others as independent responses to the importance of water.")],
 ['wiki:Vishapakar','doi:10.1038/s40494-025-01998-z','url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/tentativelists/6702|UNESCO tentative list: The Vishaps and the Cultural Landscape of Tirinkatar|official'],'Vishapakar',dateNote='Two vishaps at Tirinkatar dated c. 4200–4000 BCE; others undated'),
# --- part 3: Siberia, Mongolia and Tibet; West and Central Africa; the Andes
X('shigir-idol','The Shigir Idol: a 5-metre wooden god carved by hunter-gatherers 11,500 years ago',H,'confirmed',1,-9500,['archaeological','radiocarbon (AMS)','dendrology'],
 "The Shigir Idol is the oldest known monumental wooden sculpture. Gold prospectors' workers found it on 24 January 1890 at a depth of 4 m in the Shigir peat bog on the eastern slope of the Middle Urals, near Kirovgrad, about 100 km from Yekaterinburg. It was carved with stone tools from a single larch trunk at least 159 years old, and pulled out in ten pieces. Its flat body is covered in zigzags and lines and carries several human faces, with a head at the top. Professor D. I. Lobanov reassembled it to 2.8 m; in 1914 the archaeologist Vladimir Tolmachev used extra fragments to propose an original height of 5.3 m, though some of those pieces were later lost and survive only in his drawings. Radiocarbon dates in the 1990s gave about 9,500 years, and later German AMS dating pushed it back to about 11,500 years ago, at the very start of the Holocene, roughly twice the age of the Great Pyramid. It is in the Sverdlovsk Regional Museum of Local Lore in Yekaterinburg.",
 [("Hunter-gatherer monument","The dating means people who lived by hunting, fishing and gathering made monumental, symbolic art, like the contemporary carved pillars of Göbekli Tepe in Turkey; this challenges the older idea that complex ritual art came only after farming."),
  ("Too old to believe","When the first radiocarbon date came back, some scholars thought it must be wrong because they did not believe hunter-gatherers could make such an object; later dating proved it even older."),
  ("Coded meanings","The faces and zigzags have been read as spirits, a creation myth, a boundary marker or a map; there is no way to test these ideas, and the researchers note that wooden art of this age is almost never preserved.")],
 ['wiki:Shigir_Idol','wiki:Göbekli_Tepe'],None,dateNote='Carved c. 11,500 years ago (c. 9500 BCE); found 1890'),
X('deer-stones','Deer stones: Mongolia\'s tattooed warriors in stone with flying deer',H,'confirmed',2,-1300,['archaeological','iconographic'],
 "Deer stones are carved standing stones of granite or greenstone, found mainly in Mongolia and southern Siberia; of more than 1,500 known, over 1,300 are in Mongolia, and a few scattered examples reach as far west as the Kuban, Ukraine, Bulgaria and the Elbe. Most date from about 1400 to 700 BCE, the Late Bronze and Early Iron Age. Most are over 0.9 m tall (some reach 4.6 m), their 'face' usually turns east, and they often stand near khirigsuurs, large stone burial mounds. Each stone is a stylised human body: a belt hung with weapons and tools such as a bow case, a shield on the back, earrings and a necklace, and sometimes a face. The body is covered with elegant 'flying deer' with long beak-like snouts and swept-back antlers. The work required suggests the first complex, hierarchical societies on the Mongolian steppe, ancestors of later nomadic states.",
 [("Tattooed bodies","The flying deer may show tattoos: similar deer tattoos were found on frozen bodies of the later Pazyryk culture in the Altai, so the stones may represent real tattooed leaders."),
  ("Spirit journeys","Some scholars connect the flying deer to beliefs in deer carrying souls to the sky, later seen in Siberian shamanism; this is an interpretation."),
  ("Cut by machine?","A few stones show cuts that the Wikipedia summary describes as possibly made with a primitive mechanical drill, and the tops of some were deliberately smashed; these details are unexplained but not anomalous for metal-using societies.")],
 ['wiki:Deer_stone','wiki:Khirigsuur'],'Deer_stone',dateNote='Mostly c. 1400–700 BCE'),
X('king-gesar','King Gesar of Ling: the world\'s longest epic, still sung, and a hero who will return',M,'myth-legend',2,1100,['oral epic','textual'],
 "The Epic of King Gesar tells of Gesar, a divine being sent to earth to become the fearless king of Ling and to defeat demons and enemy kings, and to bring order to the land. Its classic form probably took shape by about the 12th century, and it spread from Tibet to Mongolia, Buryatia, Ladakh, Baltistan, Hunza and the Kalmyks. It is still performed: about 100 bards remain active in the 'Gesar belt' of China. There is no single text; a Chinese compilation of Tibetan versions fills about 120 volumes and more than a million verses, so it is often called the longest epic in the world. The first printed version was a Mongolian text published in Beijing in 1716. Instead of dying, Gesar departs to a hidden realm from which he may return to save his people.",
 [("Caesar in Tibet?","Some scholars have proposed that the name Gesar comes from the Roman title Caesar, passed via the Byzantine and Turkic world (kaiser), or via the 8th-century Turkic ruler of Kabul called Fromo Kesaro ('Caesar of Rome'); a king Phrom Ge-sar appears in some Tibetan versions. This is debated."),
  ("Prophecy","Gesar's withdrawal to a hidden realm, ready to come back, ties him to the worldwide pattern of sleeping or returning heroes such as Arthur; in some later Buddhist writings he is linked to the hidden kingdom of Shambhala.")],
 ['wiki:Epic_of_King_Gesar'],'Epic_of_King_Gesar',dateNote='Core formed by c. 12th century CE; oral tradition still alive'),
X('denisovan-tibet','The Denisovan gene that lets Tibetans breathe at altitude, and a jaw found by a meditating monk',H,'confirmed',1,-160000,['genetic','fossil','palaeoproteomic'],
 "Denisovans are an extinct group of archaic humans first identified in 2010 from DNA in a finger bone from Denisova Cave in Siberia. In 2014 geneticists showed that many Tibetans carry a version of the gene EPAS1, which helps the body cope with low oxygen, that matches the Denisovan genome; the variant probably entered modern humans through interbreeding and then spread because it helped people live on the high plateau. In 1980 a Tibetan Buddhist monk meditating in Baishiya Karst Cave, a Buddhist sanctuary high on the north-east edge of the Tibetan Plateau, found half of a human lower jaw. In 2019, analysis of ancient proteins in its teeth identified it as Denisovan, about 160,000 years old: the first Denisovan fossil found outside Denisova Cave and proof that archaic humans lived at high altitude long before modern humans. The cave is said in tradition to have been an abode of Padmasambhava.",
 [("Inherited adaptation","The mainstream view is that this is an example of adaptive introgression: modern humans picked up a useful gene from people already adapted to the environment."),
  ("Hidden people in myths","Some writers link Denisovans and other archaic humans to legends of wild men, giants and yeti-like beings in Asia; there is no evidence connecting specific legends to them."),
  ("A face for the Denisovans","In 2025, proteins and DNA linked the Harbin skull (Homo longi) to the Denisovans, which may finally show what they looked like; research is ongoing.")],
 ['wiki:Denisovan','wiki:EPAS1','wiki:Xiahe_mandible','wiki:Baishiya_Karst_Cave','doi:10.1038/nature13408','doi:10.1038/s41586-019-1139-x'],'Xiahe_mandible',dateNote='Xiahe jaw c. 160,000 years old; found 1980, identified 2019'),
X('terma-tibet','Terma: Tibetan treasure texts hidden by Padmasambhava for the future',M,'disputed',2,1350,['textual','religious tradition'],
 "In the Nyingma school of Tibetan Buddhism and in Bon, terma ('hidden treasures') are teachings believed to have been hidden in the 8th century by the master Padmasambhava and his consort Yeshe Tsogyal, to be found centuries later by treasure revealers (tertöns) when the time was right. A terma may be a physical object, such as a scroll or ritual tool buried in the ground or hidden in rocks, water or trees, or a 'mind treasure' that arises in the revealer's mind; a few symbolic letters can trigger the revealer's memory of a whole text. The most famous terma outside Tibet is the Bardo Thodol, popularly called the Tibetan Book of the Dead, revealed by Karma Lingpa in the 14th century. Similar ideas of hidden teachings recovered later exist in India, such as Nagarjuna's recovery of a sutra from the realm of the nagas.",
 [("Continuous revelation","Within the tradition, terma are genuine teachings, timed for when people are ready, and are tested by elaborate procedures; believers see this as a way the teaching stays alive."),
  ("New texts with old authority","Historians see terma as a way that new religious texts could be accepted by attributing them to an ancient master; Tibetan critics themselves sometimes questioned particular revelations."),
  ("Global pattern","Hidden scriptures recovered at the right moment appear in many traditions, from the Book of the Law 'found' in the Jerusalem Temple under King Josiah to Joseph Smith's golden plates.")],
 ['wiki:Terma_(religion)','wiki:Bardo_Thodol'],'Bardo_Thodol',dateNote='Terma tradition from c. 10th–11th centuries; Bardo Thodol revealed 14th century'),
X('kimpa-vita','Kimpa Vita: the Kongolese prophetess who said Jesus was born in Kongo',H,'confirmed',2,1704,['textual (missionary records)','historical'],
 "Dona Beatriz Kimpa Vita (1684 – 2 July 1706) was a young noblewoman of the Kingdom of Kongo, in today's Angola and DR Congo, which had been officially Catholic since the 16th century. Kongo had been torn by civil war since the Battle of Mbwila in 1665, and the old capital São Salvador (Mbanza Kongo) had been abandoned. In 1704, during an illness, she said she had died and been possessed by Saint Anthony, who sent her to reunite Kongo under one king. Her movement, Antonianism, taught that Jesus and other holy figures were Kongolese, adapted Catholic prayers, and attacked both traditional charms (nkisi) and the European missionaries; thousands of followers resettled the ruined capital. Accused of heresy, she was burned at the stake on 2 July 1706 on the orders of King Pedro IV, after he consulted Italian Capuchin friars including Bernardo da Gallo. Her movement's years are among the best documented in Kongo's history.",
 [("African Christianity","Historians such as John Thornton (The Kongolese Saint Anthony, 1998) see her as part of a long tradition of Kongolese Christianity that made the faith local; she is often compared with later Congolese prophetic movements such as Kimbanguism."),
  ("Political prophet","Some today honour her as a heroine of African resistance to European religious and colonial power; others stress that she was above all a religious reformer within Kongo's civil wars."),
  ("Sainthood and doubt","She reportedly came to doubt her own mission after becoming pregnant despite preaching chastity; the main sources are hostile Capuchin accounts, so her own voice survives only through them.")],
 ['wiki:Kimpa_Vita','wiki:Antonianism'],'Kimpa_Vita',end=1706,dateNote='Movement 1704–1706; executed 2 July 1706'),
X('senegambian-circles','Senegambian stone circles: the world\'s largest concentration of stone circles',H,'confirmed',2,-300,['archaeological'],
 "Across an area about 350 km long and 100 km wide in the Gambia and central Senegal, along the Gambia River, stand more than 1,000 stone circles and burial mounds, the largest concentration of stone circles in the world. The four main sites, Sine Ngayène and Wanar in Senegal and Wassu and Kerr Batch in the Gambia, together have around 29,000 stones in about 17,000 monuments. The stones are pillars of laterite, cut with iron tools, arranged in circles and double circles; others stand apart, usually to the east (frontal stones), and pairs of connected parallel rows of frontal stones are called lyre stones. Burials, pottery and iron objects are found in and around them. They were built over a long period, generally put between the 3rd century BCE and the 16th century CE. They were inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2006.",
 [("Who built them","There are no written records; archaeologists link them to ancestors of local peoples and to iron-using societies with chiefs or elites honoured by large funerary monuments."),
  ("Astronomy and sacred space","Some have suggested astronomical alignments, as for European stone circles; most researchers see them as funerary monuments marking a sacred landscape used for more than 1,500 years."),
  ("Parallel worlds","They are often compared with British and Breton stone circles, which are thousands of years older; the similarity is in form, not in time or culture.")],
 ['wiki:Senegambian_stone_circles','url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1226/|UNESCO World Heritage: Stone Circles of Senegambia|official'],'Senegambian_stone_circles',end=1500,dateNote='Built between c. 3rd century BCE and 16th century CE'),
X('lake-bosumtwi','Lake Bosumtwi: Ghana\'s sacred crater lake, where souls say farewell to the Earth goddess',H,'confirmed',2,-1070000,['geological','oral tradition','palaeoclimate'],
 "Lake Bosumtwi (Bosomtwe), about 30 km south-east of Kumasi, is Ghana's only natural lake. It lies in a meteorite impact crater about 10.5 km across, formed about 1.07 million years ago; tektites (glassy impact debris) thrown out by it are found in neighbouring Côte d'Ivoire. The Asante consider it sacred: in traditional belief the souls of the dead come here to bid farewell to the Earth goddess Asase Ya, and it has been considered proper to fish in the lake only from wooden planks. About 30 villages, with some 70,000 people, lie around the lake.",
 [("Legend confirmed by science","Local tradition held that the lake was once much smaller; sediment cores show that a period of low water, when forest grew in the basin, lasted until about 300 years ago."),
  ("Impact memory?","The impact happened long before modern humans, so the sacredness of the lake cannot be a memory of it; its unusual round shape and lack of outlets are enough to explain why it was seen as special.")],
 ['wiki:Lake_Bosumtwi'],'Lake_Bosumtwi',dateNote='Crater formed c. 1.07 million years ago'),
X('nsibidi','Nsibidi: the secret sign-writing of the leopard societies that crossed to Cuba and Haiti',H,'confirmed',2,1500,['ethnographic','inscriptions','textual'],
 "Nsibidi is a system of symbols, often called proto-writing, from the Cross River region of south-eastern Nigeria and south-western Cameroon, usually attributed to the Ejagham (Ekoi) people. Hundreds of pictographic and abstract signs were drawn on walls, calabashes, brass, leaves, swords, textiles and as tattoos. Its fullest and most secret forms were kept by the Ekpe (Egbo) and Mgbe 'leopard' societies, men's associations that acted as governments and courts among the Efik, Ejagham, Igbo and their neighbours; a simpler public version could be used by women. Through the Atlantic slave trade nsibidi reached Cuba and Haiti, where it developed into the anaforuana signs of the Cuban Abakuá society (which descends from Ekpe) and influenced Haitian veve symbols. The Ikom monoliths of Cross River, about 300 carved stones possibly from around 200 CE, carry designs that some researchers think may be related.",
 [("Writing or symbols","It is usually classed as pictographic; scholars debate whether some signs worked as logograms or syllabic signs, which would make it a true writing system."),
  ("How old","Its age is unknown; claims that it is many centuries or even more than a thousand years old depend mainly on links to the Ikom monoliths, which are themselves poorly dated."),
  ("Secret societies across the ocean","The survival of Ekpe/Abakuá and its signs in Cuba is one of the clearest cases of an African institution re-created in the Americas, and part of a wider pattern of secret knowledge passed through initiation.")],
 ['wiki:Nsibidi','wiki:Ikom_monoliths','wiki:Abakuá'],'Ikom_monoliths',dateNote='Age uncertain; documented from c. 1900; possibly centuries older'),
X('titicaca-offerings','Treasures under Lake Titicaca: the Khoa reef offerings and the Island of the Sun',H,'confirmed',2,750,['underwater archaeology','radiocarbon','oral tradition'],
 "Lake Titicaca, on the border of Peru and Bolivia, was sacred in Andean belief. In Inca tradition the Sun hid under a crag on the Island of the Sun during a great flood and emerged there to light the world again, and the Incas built a pilgrimage complex on the island. Divers and local people had long recovered objects from the lake, fuelling stories of sunken temples and treasure. Systematic underwater excavations around the Khoa reef, near the Island of the Sun, recovered thousands of objects: ceramic puma-shaped incense burners, remains of sacrificed young llamas, and gold, shell and stone ornaments. A 2019 study by Christophe Delaere, José Capriles and Charles Stanish showed that many of these offerings were made by the Tiwanaku state between the 8th and 10th centuries CE, centuries before the Incas, as repeated state rituals.",
 [("Religion and the first states","The authors argue that dramatic shared rituals helped Tiwanaku consolidate power and cooperation, making the reef evidence for how religion and state formation went together."),
  ("Sunken city","Some expeditions have claimed underwater walls and a submerged temple or city; archaeologists accept that lake levels changed and drowned some structures, but the dramatic 'lost city' claims are not supported by published evidence."),
  ("Flood stories","The Inca story of the Sun rising from the lake after a flood belongs to the global pattern of origin-after-flood myths, and Lake Titicaca's water level really has risen and fallen dramatically in the past.")],
 ['wiki:Lake_Titicaca','wiki:Isla_del_Sol','doi:10.1073/pnas.1820749116'],'Isla_del_Sol',dateNote='Tiwanaku offerings 8th–10th centuries CE; Inca pilgrimages 15th–16th centuries'),
]
